  "query_preview": "..\n\nA broad upper level ridge south of the area and zonal/westerly flow \naloft maintains warm and dry conditions across the area today with \ngenerally light winds. Meanwhile, upper level troughing in the\nGulf of AK will deepen today as additional energy arrives from \nthe west, setting the stage for a significant pattern change as \nthe the large scale flow amplifies and the trough digs southward \ninto the western US this weekend. Increasing west-southwest mid-\nupper level flow ahead of the approaching trough along with surface\nlow development in southern AB will lead to windy conditions \nFriday as well as a few additional degrees of warming and \nincreased fire weather concerns through Friday evening. An initial\nPacific cold front looks to move east across central/north- \ncentral MT Friday night with stronger cooling from north to south \non Saturday as an airmass from Canada surges south through the \narea. \n\nPrecipitation develops Saturday night along the continental divide \nwith the initial Pacific front with more widespread precipitation \ndeveloping across central MT Saturday, both along and following the \nsurge of colder air and in association with a plume of deeper \nmoisture and frontogenic forcing. Models are in reasonable \nagreement with the evolution of the trough this weekend as it\nsplits/elongates with much of the energy digging south along the \nwest coast through Sunday. This maintains a flow of deep moisture \nacross the area in SW flow aloft, while the lower level cold air \ncontinues to deepen and spread south through Sunday. The focus for\nsteady precipitation will likely continue across central MT into \nSaturday night then shift southward through SW MT later Saturday \nnight through Sunday. Beyond this, models diverge with how long \nprecipitation may linger but an overall decrease from NW to SE is \nlikely later Sunday through early Monday. \n\nThe combination of cooling aloft with the upper trough and surge \nof lower level cold air from the north will rapidly cool \ntemperatures and lower snow levels across western portions of \nnorth-central/central MT on Saturday with daytime temperatures \nlikely in the upper 30s to around 40 by Saturday afternoon as snow\nlevels lower to around 3000-4000 ft. While snow levels are also \nlikely to lower to near the valleys in SW MT Saturday night, \nconfidence in timing is lower. Potential exists for significant \nsnowfall amounts over some mountain passes this weekend with main \nimpacts likely in the Saturday night through Sunday morning period\nwhen snow is most likely to accumulate on surfaces that will take\ntime to cool after the recent warmth. A few inches of snow are \nalso likely at lower elevation locations including Helena and \nGreat Falls. The drastic change to colder temperatures this \nweekend will be an impact to anyone with outdoor plans, especially\nafter the recent extended period of mild weather. Winter weather \nstatements are likely for portions of the area this weekend as \nconfidence in snowfall amounts and timing increases. Hoenisch",
